@page "/Blog"
@page "/Blog/{Filter}"

<PageTitle>Blog</PageTitle>

<MudStack>
	<BlogBlock Title="Website hosting on GitHub" CreationDate=DateTime.Now Filter=@Filter>
		<BlogHeader>
			<MudText>For this project, I wanted to use Blazor to create my own website with minimal hosting coosts.</MudText>
			<MudText>This solution that you see on this page is where I am currrently with MudBlazor using Blazor WASM and hosting on GitHub pages.</MudText>
		</BlogHeader>

		<BlogBody>
			<MudText>This solution that I landed on is very nice, but it does come with some pretty noticable pros and cons.</MudText>
			<ProsConsList>
				<Pros>
					<ol type="1" style="margin-left: 20px;">
						<li><MudText>Free hosting!!</MudText></li>
						<li><MudText>GitHub Action support</MudText></li>
						<li><MudText>MudBlazor UI framework</MudText></li>
					</ol>
				</Pros>
				<Cons>
					<ol type="1" style="margin-left: 20px;">
						<li><MudText>Load times - The loading animation while cute is there to hide the time it takes for Blazor to inject itself</MudText></li>
						<li><MudText>Page navigation is technically done by rerouting off of the GitHub 404 page lol</MudText></li>
						<li><MudText>Metadata on properly works on the home page</MudText></li>
					</ol>
				</Cons>
			</ProsConsList>
			<MudText>For my use case, the hard-learnings and low-cost were perfect.</MudText>

			<MudText>How I did this</MudText>
			<ol type="1" style="margin-left: 20px;">
				<li>
					<MudText>Create a new GitHub project using a MudBlazor starting template for a WASM project.  You can follow the directions from here: 
						<MudLink Href="https://mudblazor.com/getting-started/installation#using-templates" Target="_blank">Mud Blazor Getting Started</MudLink>
					</MudText>
				</li>
				<li>
					<MudText>Set your website's base href to a handy default such as "/LocalWebsite/"</MudText>
					<MudText>My source code: <MudLink Href="https://github.com/TortoiseEatToes/PersonalWebsite/blob/main/wwwroot/index.html#L6" Target="_blank">index.html</MudLink></MudText>
					
				</li>
				<li>
					<MudText>Set your launchSettings.json properties to match the same value</MudText>
					<MudText>My source code: <MudLink Href="https://github.com/TortoiseEatToes/PersonalWebsite/blob/main/Properties/launchSettings.json#L13" Target="_blank">launchSettings.json</MudLink></MudText>
				</li>
				<li>
					<MudText>Finally, set your project up to auto-deploy to GitHub pages by following this documentation:
						<MudLink Href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/aspnet/core/blazor/host-and-deploy/webassembly/github-pages?view=aspnetcore-10.0" Target="_blank">Blazor host and depoy</MudLink>
					</MudText>
					<MudText>You can see my finished version of that action here: <MudLink Href="https://github.com/TortoiseEatToes/PersonalWebsite/blob/main/.github/workflows/static.yml" Target="_blank">static.yml</MudLink></MudText>
				</li>
				<li>
					<MudText>Finally, modify your action to meet your needs.</MudText>
					<MudText>In my case, I also added a block to update the publish tag for fun. <MudLink Href="https://github.com/TortoiseEatToes/PersonalWebsite/blob/main/.github/workflows/static.yml#L52" Target="_blank">static.yml</MudLink>
					</MudText>
				</li>
			</ol>
			<MudText>Lessons learned</MudText>
			<MudText>Bring up a code example of the base href.</MudText>
			<MudText>Mention Metadata tags.</MudText>
		</BlogBody>
	</BlogBlock>
</MudStack>

@code {
	[Parameter]
	public string? Filter { get; set; } = "SaraTest";
}
